.NET Framework is Microsoft's older development platform, mainly used to build Windows applications.

.NET Core was introduced as a modern, lightweight, cross-platform version of .NET that can run on Windows, Linux, and macOS.
2. Difference between .NET Framework and .NET Core
Feature	.NET Framework	.NET Core
Platform	Windows only	Windows, Linux, macOS
Performance	Comparatively slower in many workloads	Generally better performance
Open Source	Mostly proprietary historically	Open source
Deployment	Usually requires framework installation	Supports self-contained deployment
Microservices	Less suitable	Better support
Docker	Limited compared to modern .NET	Excellent support
Web development	ASP.NET	ASP.NET Core
Latest development	Maintenance	Modern development continues

3. Why was .NET Core introduced?
Suppose a company wants to develop a Web API.

Earlier, using .NET Framework:
- Application mainly runs on Windows.
- Hosting generally requires Windows Server and IIS.
- Deployment is less flexible.
Microsoft introduced .NET Core to solve these limitations.

With .NET Core:
- We can run applications on Windows or Linux.
- We can deploy applications inside Docker containers.
- We can build high-performance APIs.
- We can develop cloud-based applications and microservices.
4. Real-life example






















Suppose we develop a Vehicle Management Web API.

Using .NET Framework:
public class VehicleController : ApiController
{
    public string Get()
    {
        return "Vehicle API";
    }
}


This application typically runs on Windows with IIS.

Using .NET Core:
[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class VehicleController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet]
    public string Get()
    {
        return "Vehicle API";
    }
}


This API can run on:
- Windows
- Linux
- Docker containers
- Cloud platforms
Both can build Web APIs, but the hosting and development capabilities are different.
5. Interview-ready answer (2 years experience)
You can speak this answer in your interview:

Interview Answer

.NET Framework is Microsoft's older development platform which mainly supports Windows applications. It is used to develop Web applications, Windows applications and Web APIs.

.NET Core was introduced to overcome the limitations of .NET Framework, especially platform dependency and deployment flexibility.

The major advantages of .NET Core are cross-platform support, better performance, open-source nature, lightweight deployment and better support for microservices and Docker.

For example, if I develop a Web API using .NET Core, I can deploy it on both Windows and Linux servers, whereas .NET Framework applications generally require Windows.

Today, modern development continues on .NET, which evolved from .NET Core.

6. One important interview point
Remember this
.NET Core 3.1 was the last version with the name ".NET Core".

After that, Microsoft introduced .NET 5, followed by .NET 6, .NET 7, .NET 8, etc.

So today:
- .NET Framework → Older Windows-based platform.
- .NET Core → Modern cross-platform foundation.
- .NET 5+ → Continuation of .NET Core under the name .NET.
